 "transcript": {
  "answer": "I can't tell you what the cash rate will be in March 2027. The RBA board sets it at meetings, and I don't have market pricing or RBA forecasts to estimate a likely path.\n\n**Where it stands now:** The cash rate target was **4.36%** as a monthly average for September 2026, the latest figure in the RBA data. It was 3.60% through December 2025 and January 2026, then rose in four steps from February to May 2026 to 4.35%, where it has held since June.\n\n**Recent data that bears on the decision:**\n- **Trimmed mean inflation** (the RBA's preferred underlying measure) was **3.6% year-on-year in August 2026**, up from 2.8% in June 2025. That is above the 2–3% target band.\n- **Headline CPI** was **4.0% year-on-year in August 2026**.\n- **Unemployment** was **4.7% in September 2026**, up from 4.3% in June 2025. Rising unemployment points toward easing, while high inflation points toward holding or tightening.\n\n**Ways to get a better read:**\n- Cash rate futures and the ASX 30-day rate futures show what markets expect for upcoming meetings. Check those for the most direct market view.\n- The RBA publishes its meeting calendar and minutes, and its Statement on Monetary Policy gives its own forecasts.\n\nIf the rate is unchanged, March 2027 would still be 4.35%. The next moves depend on whether inflation keeps running above target, and on how far the rising unemployment rate goes.",
